    "pitch": "I want to pitch you \u2018Sunrise Static,\u2019 a psychological western told entirely through the perspective of Silas, a man known across the territory as \u2018The Serpent.\u2019 He\u2019s legendary, untouchable\u2026except, he's terrified. The film opens an hour before dawn. Silas is in a dilapidated shack, meticulously cleaning his revolver \u2013 a ritual he usually finds calming, but now it just amplifies the tremor in his hands. We see only his point of view, distorted by sweat and flickering candlelight, hearing his frantic, internal monologue overlayed with the distant, unsettling chirping of birds (sound design note: build tension with gradually increasing bird sounds). He\u2019s supposed to face Jedediah Crane at sunrise, a duel everyone\u2019s been waiting for. But the thought of stepping into the light, of facing Crane\u2019s unwavering gaze, has him frozen.  The dream shifts \u2013 a warped sequence of memories, blurring his past successes with horrifying near misses (visual note: quick, disorienting cuts \u2013 a dust devil swallowing a horse, a woman's anguished face, flashes of gunsmoke). We follow him as he tries to rationalize, to strategize, to *run*, each attempt more desperate than the last, his reputation crumbling with every tick of the clock. The question isn't about skill, it's about whether a man who\u2019s built his life on fear can finally face it, or will he vanish before the sun even rises?",
